// GOOD: Structured whiteboard style
public int findMax(int[] arr) {
    // Handle edge case
    if (arr == null || arr.length == 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Array cannot be empty");
    }

    int max = arr[0];

    // Scan remaining elements
    for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > max) {
            max = arr[i];
        }
    }

    return max;
}

// Interviewer: "Find the first duplicate in an array."

// You (out loud): "Okay, so I need to track what I've seen.
// I'll use a HashSet to store elements as I iterate.
// If I encounter an element already in the set, that's my duplicate."

public Integer findFirstDuplicate(int[] nums) {
    // You: "First, I'll handle the edge case — empty or null array."
    if (nums == null || nums.length == 0) {
        return null;
    }

    var seen = new HashSet<Integer>();

    // You: "Now I'll loop through the array."
    for (int num : nums) {
        // You: "If the set already contains this number, it's our answer."
        if (seen.contains(num)) {
            return num;
        }
        seen.add(num);
    }

    // You: "If we finish the loop, no duplicates exist."
    return null;
}

// INITIAL CODE: Bug in loop boundary
public int sumArray(int[] arr) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= arr.length; i++) {  // BUG: <= causes ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
        sum += arr[i];
    }
    return sum;
}

// You notice: "Wait, I see a problem. I'm using <= in the loop condition,
// which will access arr[arr.length] and throw an exception.
// Let me fix that."

// CORRECTED CODE
public int sumArray(int[] arr) {
    // Handle null/empty input
    if (arr == null || arr.length == 0) {
        return 0;
    }

    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {  // FIXED: < instead of <=
        sum += arr[i];
    }
    return sum;
}

// You say: "Now it correctly stops at the last valid index."

// PHASE 1: Clarify (5 min)
// You: "So we're finding duplicates in an integer array.
// Can it be empty? Can values be negative? Should I return
// the first duplicate or all duplicates?"

// PHASE 2: Plan (5 min)
// You: "I'll use a HashSet to track seen elements. Loop through
// the array, check if each element is in the set. If yes, return it.
// If no, add it. Time: O(n), Space: O(n). Does that sound good?"

// PHASE 3: Code (20 min)
public Integer findFirstDuplicate(int[] nums) {
    if (nums == null || nums.length < 2) {
        return null;  // No duplicates possible
    }

    var seen = new HashSet<Integer>();
    for (int num : nums) {
        if (seen.contains(num)) {
            return num;
        }
        seen.add(num);
    }
    return null;
}

// PHASE 4: Test (5 min)
// You: "Let me trace [2,5,2,8]. seen={}, see 2 add it, see 5 add it,
// see 2 again — it's in the set, return 2. Correct."

// PHASE 5: Complexity (5 min)
// You: "Time is O(n) because one pass. Space is O(n) worst case if no duplicates."

// CODE: Find first duplicate in an array
public Integer findFirstDuplicate(int[] nums) {
    if (nums == null || nums.length < 2) {
        return null;
    }
    var seen = new HashSet<Integer>();
    for (int num : nums) {
        if (seen.contains(num)) {
            return num;
        }
        seen.add(num);
    }
    return null;
}

